# BUGS

Identity is advisory: a spawn message's `user=` is whatever the spawner wrote, and a client names whom it attaches as, so fsd's permissions stop mistakes, not a program that names another user.
keyd, at M10, is to sign who a client is.
Until M6 step 6e, POSIX programs see no owners: `getuid` and its kin answer 0.

There are no capability tokens yet (`docs/02-namespace-swarm.md` §3.4, M10): anyone who can open `/proc/<pid>` can debug, signal and kill that process, procfs trusts the task and parent a registration names, and nsd(4) takes a caller's word for which task it is.

ADR-0029's broad grants (the grant settings, svcd's records of what each package was granted, packages' `needs=`, `/lib/ns/posix.user`) come with packages at M7; svcd reads only the boot image's manifests, and a process's `status` names no grants.
The design's `/proc/<pid>/caps`, a process's handles and their rights, is not there.

Handles carry no badge, the value a server is to tell its clients apart by (`docs/01-kernel-ipc.md` §3); each client has a connection of its own instead.
`handle_replace` is reserved; VMO leases, revocable shares of memory and leases lent for one call, come in M6 step 6e; an IoRange cannot be revoked.

Some calls check rights by trying a handle as each kind of object in turn, so a missing right is reported as `VX_ERR_BAD_HANDLE`, not `VX_ERR_ACCESS` (port(2), counter(2)).
`debug_write` is open to every task, not only to a holder of a debug capability (debug(2)).

The wall clock and power off are gated on the root Resource, the coarsest right there is (ADR-0031).
A function no IOMMU covers gets a pass-through domain, whose device reaches all of memory; VT-d remaps no interrupts, so a device's MSIs are not checked against their source (M6 step 6g).
